//? Title: De Boor's Algorithm: From a Control Polygon to a B-Spline Point
//? Subtitle: Interpolation triangles, nonuniform knots, continuity, local control and exact endpoint evaluation
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given a knot sequence and a polygon of control points, we want to evaluate a smooth
parametric curve without expanding its pieces into powers or repeatedly computing
basis functions that contribute nothing. De Boor's algorithm reduces a small active
control net through knot-weighted linear interpolation. We derive that reduction from
the B-spline basis recursion, calculate two quadratic examples by hand, and expose
every intermediate point and weight through datamunge's DeBoorSpline. Repeated knots
show the difference between a corner and a jump. Generated Bezier families check the
curve against a closed form and verify the quadratic interpolation count. A control-point
perturbation demonstrates exact local support, and the original six-control cubic
provides a larger construction. Both endpoints are evaluated directly, including on
parameter domains much smaller than an arbitrary fixed epsilon.
)

\toc

> The method

>> A parametric curve, rather than a fit through observations

Choose degree \(p\geq0\), \(N\geq p+1\) planar controls \(P_i\), and \(N+p+1\)
nondecreasing knots \(u_i\). The curve is

$$
C(t)=\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}P_i B_{i,p}(t),\qquad t\in[u_p,u_N].
$$
\alttext(A B-spline curve is a weighted sum of N planar control points, with degree-p B-spline basis weights, evaluated on the base interval from knot p to knot N.)

The parameter \(t\) is not generally the curve's horizontal coordinate or its arc
length. Interior controls guide the curve; they are not interpolation targets.
Fitting coefficients from noisy measurements is a separate problem, covered by the
P-spline report. No empirical dataset is needed here: prescribed control nets let us
check the evaluator against exact answers.

On a nonempty knot span \([u_k,u_{k+1})\), only the \(p+1\) basis functions with
indices \(k-p,\ldots,k\) can contribute. Normalized B-splines are nonnegative and
sum to one on the base interval. This representation therefore gives both local
control and a convex-hull constraint.

>> Deriving the interpolation triangle

The degree-zero basis indicates a knot interval. Higher degrees obey the recursion

$$
B_{i,q}(t)=\frac{t-u_i}{u_{i+q}-u_i}B_{i,q-1}(t)
 +\frac{u_{i+q+1}-t}{u_{i+q+1}-u_{i+1}}B_{i+1,q-1}(t).
$$
\alttext(A degree-q basis function is a knot-weighted combination of two neighbouring degree-q-minus-one functions. A term with a zero denominator contributes zero.)

Substitute this into a sum \(\sum_i Q_i B_{i,q}(t)\) and collect the coefficient
of each \(B_{i,q-1}\). It becomes
\((1-\alpha_i)Q_{i-1}+\alpha_i Q_i\), with
\(\alpha_i=(t-u_i)/(u_{i+q}-u_i)\). Repeating this degree reduction leaves one
nonzero degree-zero basis value, equal to one on the chosen span. Thus the final
coefficient is the curve point.

In local work-vector indices, initialise \(d_j^{(0)}=P_{k-p+j}\) for
\(j=0,\ldots,p\). At round \(r=1,\ldots,p\), put \(i=k-p+j\) and compute

$$
\alpha_j^{(r)}=\frac{t-u_i}{u_{i+p-r+1}-u_i},\qquad
 d_j^{(r)}=(1-\alpha_j^{(r)})d_{j-1}^{(r-1)}+\alpha_j^{(r)}d_j^{(r-1)},
 \quad j=r,\ldots,p.
$$
\alttext(Each round blends adjacent points using a weight determined by two knots. The knot interval shortens as the round number increases. The final surviving point is d p at round p.)

The answer is \(d_p^{(p)}\). Updating \(j\) from \(p\) *down* to \(r\) lets the
same work vector hold successive rounds: the left parent still belongs to the old
round when it is read. An ascending update would consume a modified parent and
usually give a different answer. Trace tables retain the actual descending update order.
The method follows [de Boor's original work|https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9045(72)90080-9].

Each active denominator spans the selected nonempty interval, so it is positive even
when other knots coincide. On that span all weights lie in \([0,1]\). The curve
therefore stays inside the convex hull of the active controls and reproduces a constant
control net. For an affine map \(F(P)=AP+b\), interpolation commutes with \(F\):
transforming the controls transforms the curve by exactly the same map in exact arithmetic.

>> Endpoints, repeated knots and what stability means

The library selects the right-hand span at an interior knot. At the final domain endpoint
it evaluates the last span's left limit directly. Repeating each boundary knot \(p+1\)
times makes the endpoints equal to the first and last controls. Unclamped splines need
not interpolate either control. There is no extrapolation in `DeBoorSpline`.

An interior knot of multiplicity \(m\leq p\) guarantees at least \(C^{p-m}\)
continuity; special controls can make it smoother. Multiplicity \(p+1\) allows a jump.
A curve with such knots should not be drawn as one polyline across all sampled values.
`plot_curve()` draws separate polynomial spans, including their individual left-limit
endpoints, so no line is invented across a discontinuity.

For fixed knots and parameter, write \(w_i=B_{i,p}(t)\). If each control is perturbed
by at most \(\varepsilon\) in a chosen norm, then

$$
\|\Delta C(t)\|=\left\|\sum_iw_i\Delta P_i\right\|
 \leq\sum_iw_i\|\Delta P_i\|\leq\varepsilon.
$$
\alttext(Nonnegative weights summing to one bound the curve perturbation by the largest control-point perturbation.)

This is an absolute conditioning statement with respect to controls. It does not promise
small relative error when a coordinate nearly cancels to zero, or insensitivity to
perturbing very close knots. Floating-point evaluation also rounds the knot ratios and
interpolations. The implementation uses bounded blends and scales a knot ratio if its
full interval width would overflow; it cannot recover distinctions already lost when
knots are rounded to the same floating-point number.

>> Work and library interfaces

There are \(p+(p-1)+\cdots+1=p(p+1)/2\) point interpolations per evaluation.
A validated `DeBoorSpline` locates the span by binary search, then evaluates in
\(O(\log N+p^2)\) work with \(O(p)\) scratch. Construction validates and owns the
inputs in \(O(N+p)\). A full trace stores \((p+1)(p+2)/2\) initial/intermediate
points and takes \(O(p^2)\) space. The compatibility function `de_boor()` constructs
a validated object for every call, so repeated work should use `DeBoorSpline`.

Inputs must be finite, knots must have the stated count and order, multiplicity must
not exceed \(p+1\), and both domain-end spans must be nonempty. Out-of-domain parameters
and sample counts below two are rejected. `sample()` includes both exact endpoints
and is uniform in parameter, not arc length. No fixed epsilon is subtracted from the
right endpoint; on a domain such as \([0,10^{-15}]\), subtracting \(10^{-12}\) would
move the last sample outside the domain altogether.

> Simple worked examples

>> A quadratic Bezier: all weights equal one quarter

Use degree two, controls \((0,0),(2,4),(4,0)\), and knots
\((0,0,0,1,1,1)\). There is one polynomial piece, a quadratic Bezier curve.
At \(t=1/4\), the first round produces

$$
 d_1^{(1)}=(1/2,1),\qquad d_2^{(1)}=(5/2,3),\qquad
 C(1/4)=\tfrac34(1/2,1)+\tfrac14(5/2,3)=(1,3/2).
$$
\alttext(The first round gives one half, one and five halves, three. Blending these again with right weight one quarter gives the curve point one, three halves.)

All three interpolation weights are one quarter, so de Boor reduces to de Casteljau.
The trace includes the three original controls, the two first-round points and the final
point. Initial rows have undefined weights, shown as NaN rather than as artificial zeros.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/de_boor_quadratic.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const dm::geometry::DeBoorSpline spline(2,{0,0,0,1,1,1},{{0,0},{2,4},{4,0}});
  const auto trace=spline.trace(.25);
  auto construction=trace.plot(), curve=spline.plot_curve();
  construction.title("Three controls, two rounds, one point").y_limits(-.4,6.5);
  curve.title("A single quadratic Bezier piece").y_limits(-.4,6.5)
    .points({trace.point.x},{trace.point.y},"t = 1/4",{220,90,35},6);
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(construction).add(curve);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/de_boor_quadratic.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",trace.table().select({"round","local","alpha","x","y"}).to_md(6));
}
```
