\caption(The exact quadratic construction at one quarter. The final point lies on the first-round segment and inside the active control triangle. Coordinate aspect ratios are illustrative, not a distance scale.)
\alttext(Left: a grey triangle joins the three controls, an orange segment joins the first-round points, and a green point marks the answer at one, one and a half. Right: the same point is marked on the quadratic curve below the control polygon.)

>> Nonuniform knots: the weights depend on the round and index

Add control \((6,2)\) and use knots \((0,0,0,1,3,3,3)\). At \(t=1/2\), span
\(k=2\) still uses only the first three controls, but the first-round weights now differ:

$$
\alpha_1^{(1)}=1/2,\quad \alpha_2^{(1)}=1/6,\quad
 d_1^{(1)}=(1,2),\quad d_2^{(1)}=(7/3,10/3),\quad
\alpha_2^{(2)}=1/2,\quad C(1/2)=(5/3,8/3).
$$
\alttext(The first two weights are one half and one sixth, producing points one, two and seven thirds, ten thirds. The final weight is one half, giving five thirds, eight thirds.)

Using the parameter itself as every interpolation weight would be wrong on this knot
sequence. The table shows the actual lower and upper knots used at each step.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/de_boor_nonuniform.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const dm::geometry::DeBoorSpline spline(2,{0,0,0,1,3,3,3},{{0,0},{2,4},{4,0},{6,2}});
  const auto trace=spline.trace(.5);
  auto construction=trace.plot(), curve=spline.plot_curve();
  construction.title("Knot weights: 1/6, 1/2, then 1/2").y_limits(-.4,6.5);
  curve.title("Two spans of unequal parameter width").y_limits(-.4,6.5)
    .points({trace.point.x},{trace.point.y},"t = 1/2",{220,90,35},6);
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(construction).add(curve);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/de_boor_nonuniform.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",trace.table().to_md(6));
}
```
