\caption(The original cubic, now evaluated at the actual right endpoint and accompanied by its complete construction triangle. Four active controls become three, two and one points.)
\alttext(Left: a smooth cubic runs from zero, zero to eight, zero, guided by its six-control polygon; the parameter-one-and-a-half point is marked. Right: successive interpolation polygons converge to four, three point zero six two five.)

> Conclusion

The basis recursion explains both the algorithm and its geometric guarantees. A fixed
span needs only a small control net, descending updates preserve the previous round,
and convex weights give local control, affine equivariance and an absolute perturbation
bound. The hand calculations distinguish Bezier weights from nonuniform knot weights.
Repeated knots make the side convention and continuity guarantees essential.

The generated families verify exact polynomial expectations and the \(p(p+1)/2\)
interpolation count. The moved-control experiment verifies a whole support interval,
not just one visually similar curve. Endpoint evaluation now uses the requested
parameter directly. Uniform sampling remains a rendering choice, and stable evaluation
does not solve fitting, knot selection or arc-length sampling.

> References

- C. de Boor (1972). *On calculating with B-splines.* Journal of Approximation Theory 6(1), 50–62. [Original paper|https://doi.org/10.1016/0021-9045(72)90080-9].
- C. de Boor. [A Practical Guide to Splines: author's code|https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~deboor/pgs/], including the basis and spline evaluators.
- SciPy. [BSpline documentation|https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.interpolate.BSpline.html], for base-interval and coefficient conventions and an independent numerical implementation.
